\parameters (
	size:"22pt"
	colour:"red"
)

\procedure spans-its-signature(
	a:"one"
	b:"""a triple-quoted value
carried to a second line"""
)
A body.
\end

<<spans-its-arguments a:"""an argument
carried across a line""">>

<$button class="a value
carried across a line">a double-quoted attribute</$button>

<$button class='a value
carried across a line'>a single-quoted attribute</$button>

<div>
<![CDATA[
a character-data payload across lines
]]>
</div>

<script>
/* a block comment
carried across lines */
var afterTheComment = 1; // a line comment
</script>

\define spans-its-pragma-signature(a:"one"
b:"two")
A macro body.
\end

\procedure carries-four-quotings(a:"""a triple-quoted default
""" b:'a single-quoted default
' c:[[a bracketed default
]] d:((a variable default
)))
A body.
\end

A paragraph carrying <<spans-its-arguments a:"one"
b:"two">> mid-line.

<$button filter={{{ [tag[done]]
}}}>a filtered attribute</$button>

<$button field={{a title
!!a field}}>an indirect attribute</$button>

<$button v=((a variable
name))>a variable attribute</$button>

<$button title=[[a bracketed
value]]>a bracketed attribute</$button>

<$button title=`a substituted
value`>a substituted attribute</$button>

<?xml version="1.0"
standalone="yes"?>

<math><notanelement
>an element name no grammar claims</notanelement></math>

<script type="text/x-unknown">
a payload under a type no grammar claims
</script>

A PRAGMA ANSWERS ONLY ABOVE PROSE, so the explanation this carrier owes its reader stands INSIDE
the first definition's body rather than above it. Every construct here opens on one line and closes
on another, which is the position a region has to survive.
